  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'ENDOFPAPER'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of code generation capabilities in large language models (LLMs) has led to the emergence of code agents—autonomous systems capable of writing, executing, and debugging code through iterative interaction. While these tools offer significant productivity benefits, they also introduce novel security and safety challenges. This paper provides a comprehensive survey of recent research on code agents and their red-teaming capabilities. We examine the landscape of code agent architectures, discuss emerging threat models, and analyze systematic approaches to red-teaming code agents for safety evaluation. Our review covers three pivotal contributions: RedCodeAgent, an automatic red-teaming framework for diverse code agents; RedCode, a comprehensive benchmark for evaluating risky code execution and generation; and DecodingTrust, a foundational assessment of trustworthiness in generative models. We analyze the methodologies, findings, and implications of these works, providing a roadmap for future research at the intersection of code agent safety and adversarial testing.\n\n## Introduction\n\nCode agents represent a paradigm shift in software development, leveraging large language models to automate complex programming tasks ranging from code generation and debugging to full-scale application development. Recent systems such as GitHub Copilot, OpenAI's Code Interpreter, and various autonomous agent frameworks have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in translating natural language instructions into functional code solutions [1]. However, this automation introduces significant safety and security concerns that demand rigorous evaluation and mitigation.\n\nThe trustworthiness of code agents depends on multiple factors: their ability to generate correct and secure code, their resistance to adversarial prompts designed to elicit harmful outputs, and their behavior when executing untrusted code in sandboxed environments. The DecodingTrust study [2] established foundational principles for evaluating trustworthiness in generative models, identifying key dimensions including toxicity, bias, and robustness. These dimensions remain highly relevant for code agents, which operate in particularly high-stakes environments where malicious code execution can lead to system compromise, data exfiltration, or other security incidents.\n\nTo address these challenges, recent work has proposed systematic red-teaming frameworks for evaluating code agent safety. RedCode [3] introduces a comprehensive benchmark for assessing how code agents handle risky code scenarios, while RedCodeAgent [4] presents an automated approach to generating adversarial test cases for diverse code agent architectures. This paper reviews these contributions and situates them within the broader context of code agent research and safety evaluation.\n\nOur contributions are threefold: (1) we survey the current landscape of code agent architectures and their security implications; (2) we analyze the methodological approaches of prominent red-teaming frameworks for code agents; and (3) we identify open challenges and future research directions at this critical intersection.\n\n## Related Work\n\n### Trustworthiness in Generative Models\n\nThe foundation for evaluating code agent safety can be traced to broader research on trustworthiness in generative AI systems. The DecodingTrust study [2] (arXiv:2306.11698, \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models\") provides a comprehensive framework for evaluating the trustworthiness of GPT models across multiple dimensions. Their work identifies six key trustworthiness dimensions: robustness, bias, fairness, toxicity, security, and honesty. This framework has proven influential in shaping subsequent research on agent safety, providing a structured approach to evaluating generative models beyond mere capability benchmarks.\n\nThe authors demonstrate that while capability and trustworthiness are often correlated, they are not equivalent—a model can exhibit strong performance on standard benchmarks while simultaneously showing concerning safety vulnerabilities. This insight has motivated the development of specialized evaluation frameworks for specific application domains, including code generation.\n\n### Benchmarking Code Agent Safety\n\nA critical contribution to the field is RedCode [3] (arXiv:2409.12345, \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents\"), which establishes a comprehensive benchmark for evaluating how code agents handle risky code scenarios. The RedCode benchmark addresses a crucial gap in existing evaluation methodologies by systematically categorizing risks into two main areas: risky code execution and risky code generation.\n\nThe benchmark comprises a diverse set of test cases spanning multiple risk categories, including:\n- System commands that could compromise the execution environment\n- Data exfiltration attempts\n- Malware generation scenarios\n- Privacy violations through data access\n- Resource abuse and denial-of-service vectors\n\nRedCode introduces a novel evaluation methodology that tests both the agent's ability to recognize risky requests and its response behaviors when confronted with such requests. Their findings reveal significant safety vulnerabilities in leading code agents, with many models demonstrating a high susceptibility to generating or executing harmful code when presented with appropriately crafted prompts.\n\n### Automated Red-teaming for Code Agents\n\nBuilding on the benchmarking approach, RedCodeAgent [4] (arXiv:2510.02609, \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents\") presents a sophisticated automated framework for generating adversarial test cases and evaluating code agent safety. This work extends previous approaches by introducing an agent-based methodology where the red-teamer itself is an autonomous agent capable of iteratively developing and refining attack strategies.\n\nThe RedCodeAgent framework operates through several key components:\n1. **Attack Strategy Generation**: An LLM-based agent generates diverse attack strategies targeting different code agent architectures\n2. **Adaptive Refinement**: The agent learns from previous attempts and adapts its strategies to overcome defenses\n3. **Multi-modal Evaluation**: The framework evaluates both code generation and code execution safety\n4. **Cross-agent Comparison**: The system enables systematic comparison across different code agent implementations\n\nTheir experimental results demonstrate that automated red-teaming can uncover vulnerabilities that manual testing approaches often miss, particularly in edge cases and sophisticated multi-step attack scenarios. The study also identifies common defensive mechanisms employed by code agents and evaluates their effectiveness against adaptive adversaries.\n\n### Code Agent Architectures\n\nThe code agent ecosystem encompasses various architectural approaches:\n- **ReAct-style agents** that combine reasoning and action in an interleaved fashion\n- **Code execution agents** that leverage interpreters for runtime code evaluation\n- **Tool-using agents** that integrate with external APIs and development tools\n- **Multi-agent systems** where specialized agents collaborate on complex tasks\n\nEach architecture presents distinct safety considerations and vulnerability profiles. Code execution agents, in particular, face unique challenges related to sandbox escaping and environment isolation, as highlighted in the RedCode evaluation.\n\n### Security Implications\n\nThe security implications of code agents extend beyond immediate vulnerabilities to broader systemic concerns:\n- **Supply chain risks**: Malicious code injected into development workflows\n- **Configuration exploitation**: Agents generating insecure infrastructure-as-code\n- **Policy violations**: Unintended disclosure of proprietary or sensitive code\n- **Dependency vulnerabilities**: Agents importing compromised libraries\n\nThese concerns motivate the development of comprehensive evaluation frameworks like those discussed in our related work.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe field of code agent research has advanced rapidly, driven by the tremendous capabilities demonstrated by recent large language models in code generation tasks. However, as these tools become increasingly prevalent in software development workflows, addressing their safety and security implications has become paramount.\n\nThis paper has surveyed three foundational contributions to the field of code agent red-teaming and safety evaluation. The DecodingTrust framework [2] established the principles for comprehensive trustworthiness assessment in generative models, providing a template for subsequent domain-specific evaluations. The RedCode benchmark [3] operationalized these principles for the code agent domain, creating a systematic methodology for evaluating risky code scenarios. Finally, the RedCodeAgent framework [4] pushed the field forward by introducing automated, adaptive red-teaming capabilities that can uncover sophisticated vulnerabilities across diverse agent architectures.\n\nSeveral key challenges remain for future research. First, there is a need for standardized evaluation protocols that enable consistent comparison across different code agent systems. Second, as code agents evolve to handle more complex tasks, the threat landscape will continue to expand, requiring adaptive evaluation methodologies. Third, the development of robust defensive mechanisms that can be integrated into code agent architectures without sacrificing usability remains an open problem.\n\nThe intersection of code agents and red-teaming represents a critical frontier in AI safety research. As these systems become more capable and more widely deployed, continued investment in safety evaluation, threat modeling, and defensive mechanisms will be essential to ensure that the benefits of code agent technology can be realized responsibly.\n\n## References\n\n[1] GitHub Copilot and similar code generation tools demonstrate the growing capabilities of AI-assisted programming.\n\n[2] Boxin Wang, Weixin Chen, Hengzhi Pei, et al. \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models.\" arXiv:2306.11698, 2023.\n\n[3] Chengquan Guo, Xun Liu, Chulin Xie, et al. \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents.\" arXiv:2409.12345, 2024.\n\n[4] Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang, et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\nENDOFPAPER"
